6. Security & Compliance Layer

6.1 Threat Model

Defends against: prompt injection, privilege escalation, resource exhaustion, data exfiltration via export, schema reconnaissance.

6.2 Policy Enforcement Engine (AST‑based)

· Uses sqlglot to parse every generated SQL into an AST.
· Forbids: DROP, DELETE, UPDATE, INSERT, TRUNCATE, ALTER, CREATE, GRANT, REVOKE, EXEC, EXECUTE, stored procedure calls.
· Enforced at application layer + dual‑enforced by read‑only DB user.
· Row‑level security: rewrites queries to add WHERE department_id = :user_dept before execution.

6.3 Audit Schema & Data Minimisation (Differential Audit)

Problem: “zero‑persistence” conflicts with “7‑year audit retention”. A full SQL log lets an attacker replay queries to reconstruct the dataset. Solution: differential audit with separate retention policies.

What is logged? What is never stored? Enterprise option
User ID, session ID, timestamp, prompt text (hashed), execution cost, rows returned, schema version, policy decisions Actual query result rows, full SQL text (by default) Full SQL log (encrypted, with separate access control), configurable per role
Cryptographic hash of the generated SQL (SHA‑256) for non‑repudiation Session UI state SQL fingerprint only → satisfies SOC 2 without exposing PHI

GDPR Article 17 (right to erasure) is honoured: when a user deletes their account, their prompt hashes and session logs are purged according to policy, while anonymised aggregates for compliance remain.

Export controls enforce tiered policies: viewer = no export; analyst = watermarked CSV, max 500 rows; admin = unrestricted, fully logged.

